# --- Secrets: the whole point of this file. .env holds 15 Gemini keys,
# --- Supabase service-role key, and Gmail credentials. Never committed.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# The e2e build's config (apps/web/.env.e2e): a public project URL and an unsigned
# placeholder anon JWT, no secret. Committed so CI and every machine build the same bundle.
!.env.e2e

# --- Playwright browser session state. Contains live logged-in cookies
# --- for ATS accounts; leaking these is equivalent to leaking passwords.
worker/.auth/
**/storageState*.json
*.har

# --- Generated resume PDFs and screenshots. Contain personal data
# --- (phone, address, CTC) and are reproducible from the DB anyway.
artifacts/
**/*.generated.pdf
worker/screenshots/

# --- Dependencies and build output
node_modules/
dist/
build/
.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo

# --- Supabase CLI local state (includes local DB credentials)
supabase/.temp/
supabase/.branches/

# --- Vercel local link metadata (contains project + org ids)
.vercel/

# --- Editor / OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
.vscode/

# --- This repo's stray artifact, already present
bash.exe.stackdump

# --- Playwright run output. Screenshots of part-filled application forms carry
# --- personal data (CTC, notice period, contact details); reports are rebuilt
# --- on every run, so nothing here is worth versioning.
playwright-report/
test-results/
blob-report/
.playwright/

# Playwright MCP writes console logs and page SNAPSHOTS here, at the repo root,
# whenever anyone drives a real browser. Seven such files appeared during the
# human-lane verification and were untracked-but-visible, so a `git add -A`
# would have committed browser snapshots of Teja's console — constraint #11
# territory, arriving through a tool nobody chose to run.
#
# The hazard worth naming is the COMBINATION, not either half: a tool that writes
# into the working tree, plus staging that sweeps whatever it finds. Each is fine
# alone. This is the second instance in that family; the first swept a
# deliberately-sabotaged security guard mid-mutation into a commit. Ignoring the
# path removes one half so the combination cannot fire.
.playwright-mcp/

# Belt and braces only: the worker has NO build step — it uses .ts import
# specifiers and runs under Node 24's native type stripping, because
# tsconfig.base.json sets noEmit (required by allowImportingTsExtensions), so
# tsc emits nothing and this directory is never created today. Listed so that
# re-adding a build step cannot quietly start committing compiled output.
worker/dist/

# THE MASTER RESUME, and anything shaped like it at the repo root.
#
# Teja dropped `master resume.pdf` here at 21:14 as the ingest source. It is the
# single most sensitive file in the project — phone, address, CTC, the lot — and
# it matched NO existing rule: `artifacts/` is a directory and
# `**/*.generated.pdf` only covers rendered output, not a hand-authored source.
#
# Ignored rather than committed even though the repo is private and PROJECT_PLAN
# says it "holds resume, CTC, personal answers". Private is not the same as safe:
# a private repo can be made public, forked into an org, or handed to a
# collaborator, and git history is the one artifact here that cannot be edited
# after the fact without a force-push that five concurrent sessions are forbidden
# from performing. The asymmetry decides it — ignoring costs nothing functionally
# because the ingest reads the file from DISK, while committing it is permanent.
#
# NOT a substitute for the real rule: stage by explicit path. This removes one
# half of the write-into-tree + sweep-whatever-you-find combination that has now
# fired three times in this repo.
*.pdf
!worker/fixtures/**/*.pdf
!packages/**/fixtures/**/*.pdf

# --- Third-party reference checkouts, harvested from but never vendored.
#
# `reference/ai-job-search-upstream` is a clone of github.com/MadsLorentzen/
# ai-job-search, kept on disk so its rubrics and adapter patterns can be read
# and PORTED. It is 236 files and carries its own `.git` directory.
#
# Ignored for three separate reasons, any one of which would be sufficient:
#
# 1. It is a nested git repository. Staging it produces a gitlink (a bare commit
#    SHA pointing at a submodule that is not configured), so the files appear to
#    be committed while the tree holds nothing a fresh clone could restore.
#    That failure is silent and survives review, which is the worst combination.
# 2. It is MIT-licensed code owned by someone else. Porting a rubric under
#    attribution is a different act from committing their tree into a private
#    repo that holds Teja's resume, CTC and personal answers; keeping the two
#    physically separate keeps the provenance question answerable later.
# 3. Five sessions share ONE working tree, ONE index and ONE `.git` here. An
#    untracked 236-file directory is exactly the shape that `git add -A` sweeps
#    into an unrelated commit — the mechanism that has already fired three times
#    in this repo (a sabotaged security guard mid-mutation-test, Playwright MCP
#    snapshots at the repo root, and 392 lines of PROJECT_PLAN).
#
# The rule this does NOT replace: stage by explicit path. This removes the
# write-into-tree half of the combination so a mistake on the staging half
# cannot reach a commit.
reference/
# CI test-step output (ci.yml tees each node --test run to <pkg>-tests.log). Running those
# steps locally drops eight of these at the repo root; they are throwaway output, never source.
# Added 2026-11-04 after eight stale copies from 2026-11-02 sat untracked for two days.
*-tests.log
